"question_type":"conceptual","question":"For matrices with both $AB$ and $BA$ defined, which always holds even when $AB\\neq BA$?","options":["$\\det(AB)=0$","$\\operatorname{tr}(AB)=0$","$\\operatorname{tr}(AB)=\\operatorname{tr}(BA)$","$AB=BA$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3b4675e698c6dfcd30a45561","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch10_trace_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&100\\\\-50&2\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\operatorname{tr}(A)$?","options":["$3$","$150$","$-47$","$53$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e0c243ec16de8bc44ce0c977","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch10_trace_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why should trace not be used as a test for invertibility?","options":["Trace zero may be invertible; nonzero trace may be singular","Invertibility is determined only by off-diagonal entries.","Trace is not defined for square matrices.","A nonzero trace always means determinant zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9dacaa12b45454e3ec1c682a","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch10_trace_019_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $2\\times2$ matrix has trace $5$ and determinant $6$. What are its eigenvalues?","options":["$-2$ and $-3$","$1$ and $6$","$5$ and $6$","$2$ and $3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9502127f0220fd4c1bbb3601","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch10_trace_020_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Square matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{B}$ of the same size have $\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A})=3$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{B})=-5$. What is $\\operatorname{tr}(2\\boldsymbol{A}-\\boldsymbol{B})$?","options":["$1$","$11$","$-2$","$8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"502976b167a5de34b40564e7","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_trace_a1bacf5d_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}$, $\\boldsymbol{B}$, $\\boldsymbol{C}$ be arbitrary real $n\\times n$ matrices with $n\\ge2$. Using that $\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{X}\\boldsymbol{Y})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{Y}\\boldsymbol{X})$ for all $n\\times n$ matrices $\\boldsymbol{X}$, $\\boldsymbol{Y}$, which equality always holds?","options":["$\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{C})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{C}\\boldsymbol{B})$","$\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{C})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{C}\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{C})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{C}\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})$","$\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{C})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A})\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{C})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f92617d5b7123252c9c8a88","time_limit":30}]}}